title: LSP Hover - (Example)

\function lsp.pages() [tag[LSP]]
\procedure lsp.pair(a:"A", b:"B") a=<<a>> b=<<b>>
\function lsp.fpair(a:"A", b:"B") [[a=]addsuffix<a>] [[b=]addsuffix<b>] +[join[ ]]
\procedure lsp.outer()
	\procedure lsp.depth(where:"nested") <<where>>
	<<lsp.depth>>
\end lsp.outer
\procedure lsp.depth(where:"top") <<where>>

Open this tiddler's `.tid` file in your editor and rest the cursor on each part below. Results were checked against this wiki; they change as you add tiddlers.

!! A filter

These are the same filter written three ways, and each reports the same 5 tiddlers: a filtered transclusion, a widget attribute, and the `\function` at the top of this tiddler.

{{{ [tag[LSP]] }}}

<$list filter="[tag[LSP]]"><$link/> </$list>

!! The four outcomes

|! Hover the filter in |! You should see |
|`{{{ [tag[LSP]] }}}` |''5 tiddlers'', named |
|`{{{ [tag[LSP }}}` |''Unfinished filter'', not run |
|`{{{ [tag[a]xyz] }}}` |''Filter error:'' Missing [ in filter expression |
|`{{{ [tag[no-such-tag]] }}}` |''Matches nothing'' |

{{{ [tag[LSP }}}

{{{ [tag[a]xyz] }}}

{{{ [tag[no-such-tag]] }}}

!! A widget

Rest on `<$list` in the second filter above to see the widget, the module defining it, each attribute, and what it renders.

!! A macro call

<<list-links [tag[LSP]]>>

Rest on `list-links` to see where it is defined, what each parameter is worth here, and the tiddlers its filter lists.

!! How arguments bind

Both calls pass `a` by name and `"y"` by position, yet a procedure and a function bind them differently, exactly as ~TiddlyWiki does:

<<lsp.pair a:"x" "y">>

<<lsp.fpair a:"x" "y">>

|! Hover the name in |! You should see |
|`<<lsp.pair a:"x" "y">>` |''procedure'': `b` is `B` by default, and `y` is listed as ''ignored'' |
|`<<lsp.fpair a:"x" "y">>` |''function'': `b` is `y`, given by position |

!! A nested definition

`lsp.depth` is defined twice at the top of this tiddler: inside `lsp.outer`, and on its own. Each call resolves to the definition around it:

<<lsp.outer>> <<lsp.depth>>

|! Hover |! You should see |
|`<<lsp.depth>>` inside `\procedure lsp.outer()` |`where`, default `nested` |
|the second call on the line above |`where`, default `top` |

!! Operators and run prefixes

{{{ [tag[LSP Capabilities]!is[system]] :else[[none]] +[limit[3]] }}}

{{{ [tag[LSP Capabilities]toc-link[no]] }}}

|! Hover |! You should see |
|`tag` in the first filter |''filter operator'' `tag`, its module, its tiddlywiki.com page and its module's description, then the filter's result |
|`is` |''filter operator'' `is`, negated by `!` |
|`:else` |''run prefix'' `:else`: runs only when the output so far is empty |
|`+` |''run prefix'' `+`, short for `:and` |
|`toc-link` in the second filter |''Not a filter operator'': the field test `[field:toc-link[no]]` instead |

!! Pragma lines

|! Hover |! You should see |
|`\function` in the first line of this tiddler |what the keyword does |
|`lsp.pair` in its `\procedure` line |''procedure'' `lsp.pair`, defined here, local to this tiddler, with its parameters, defaults and call count |
|`b` in `lsp.pair(a:"A", b:"B")` |''parameter'' `b` of `lsp.pair`, default `B`, named once in its body |
|`\procedure` in the line below |''Not a definition here'': after text, it is text |

